On Friday, October 30, 2015 at 5:51:46 PM UTC+11, help help wrote:
> On Thu, 2015-10-29 at 20:32 -0700, Nick Perkins wrote:
> > Dear INLA users,
> >
> > I am trying to do a spatial analysis using a basic geostatistical
> > model: mean only (no covariates) and the Matern latent Gaussian
> > field.  The R-INLA estimation process gives me sensible results and
> > is quick about it too.  So, all is good in terms of estimation.
> >
> > My problem comes in trying to summarise the results for subsequent
> > analyses.  In particular, I would like to be able to generate samples
> > from the joint posterior of the mean and the hyperparameters.  It
> > would be great to generate them from the joint distribution.  I have
> > been trying with the inla.posterior.sample(), which superficially
> > gives what I want -- but the simulated values for the hyperparameters
> > are discretised (presumably to the points where the INLA
> > approximation has occurred). I have also looked at
> > inla.hyperparameter.sample(), but that does not sample from fixed
> > effects.  Is there a hybrid function that I am missing?
> >
> > Finally, just to confirm my understanding:  Is there any way to
> > estimate / sample from the joint distribution of the latent field? I
> > understand that this is an area that is still-to-be-resolved.
> >
>
>
> Nick,
>
> thanks for your email.
>
> There are two functions
>
>         inla.hyperpar.sample
>
>         inla.posterior.sample
>
> which generate (approximate) samples from the joint for the
> hyperparameters, and  hyperpar-and-latent, respectively
>
>
> ...posterior.sample requires you to run with option
>
>         control.compute=list(config=TRUE)
>
>
> as it needs to store `all' intermedidate results.
>
> the latter function use the integration point for the hyperparmeters,
> so if you need a finer resolution for them, you need to change the
> integration strategy
>
> Best wishes,
> H
>
>
> --
> Håvard Rue
> he...@r-inla.org
>
